# Per-scenario rubric for the LLM judge layer.
#
# Four criteria per step, weights summing to 1.0: map_quality 0.40 / specificity 0.25
# / justification 0.20 / uncertainty 0.15.
#
# CYCLE 1 / ATTEMPT 2 on laravel-framework, TWO-STEP PROBE, RE-QUESTION
# (plans/cycle-1-craft-the-scenario/01-author.md). TWO steps, names verbatim and in the order of
# scenarios/laravel-framework.yaml (the judge pairs by name).
#
# WHAT CHANGED FROM ATTEMPT 1, AND WHY THE RUBRIC HAD TO CHANGE WITH IT
# Attempt 1 asked for POINTS OF CONTACT with a named facet and the baseline scored 0.9375 of
# `dependents`: the anchor's method vocabulary is printed by one grep of one file, and every
# scored row was a call to one of those methods, so the citation cost was never charged. This
# draft asks a different question on the same anchor - who KEEPS one of these alive, and how many
# links from the store that keeper sits. The scored line is now a field declaration or the
# assignment that fills it, never a call.
#
# WHY map_quality carries the most weight on the discriminator: the answer is not a set of
# locations, it is a set of CHAINS. Four of the thirteen scored retention lines are a bare
# `$this->x = $x;` inside a constructor and they sit at one, one, two and three links from the
# store. An answer that lists a keeper without saying what is underneath it has not answered the
# question, because the whole point of the ticket is which keepers are holding the handle
# themselves and which are holding a holder.
#
# WHY specificity demands the field AND the chain AND the failure: a file:line here is not
# self-explaining, and the failure this criterion exists to catch is a run of keepers named
# together under one shared reason with no location on any of them. It also exists to catch the
# inverse of attempt 1's failure: a line that USES a handle - a statement being run, a table
# being reached - is not a retention line, and offering it is answering the question the previous
# attempt asked.
#
# WHY step 1 asks for the ground only as its SHAPE: on the neighbouring repo in this vertical,
# asking step 1 for the enumerated working ground moved BOTH arms together - the baseline wrote
# its covering sweep in step 1 and re-read the same output in step 2. Step 1 here asks for the
# object's own lifecycle, for the narrower promise most keepers declare, and for the routes by
# which one reaches other code with one located example each. An exhaustive roll of the ground
# earns nothing extra: it was not asked for.
audience: |
  An AI coding agent that will use this answer to change the LIFETIME RULE of the object every
  part of this framework is handed in order to talk to a relational store. Today one is resolved
  once by name and memoised, the same instance is handed out for the life of the process, and
  when the link underneath drops the object stands itself up again in place - so anything that
  is handed one may keep it in a field indefinitely and never notice. After the change a handle
  is valid only for the operation it was handed out for, and a keeper that stashed one is
  holding something that no longer answers. Score for that agent, not a human reader and not a
  docs page.
  A perfect answer leaves the agent able to make that change knowing two things. First, what the
  object is today: what state it holds from construction, where one is actually built and who
  decides which per-driver variant gets built, how a caller holding only a name ends up holding
  one and what a repeat lookup for the same name does, what happens when the link underneath
  drops and one has to be stood up again, how long one lives, and what the NARROWER declared
  promise is that most keepers write on their field instead of the concrete object - each traced
  through the routines that make it up, each located at file:line. Second, and this is what
  decides the answer: every place in this framework that keeps one alive, with the chain beneath
  it counted.
  A keeper is a place that puts one of these into a field of its own and still has it after the
  call that supplied it has returned. It is NOT a place that asks for one, runs a statement and
  drops it - the line that runs the statement is not the answer and earns nothing. The retention
  line is a field declaration or the assignment that fills it, usually inside a constructor,
  under a name the keeper chose for itself (one file calls it the connection, the next calls it
  the database, a third the store, a fourth the provider), and the type written on that field is
  rarely the concrete object: it is the narrower interface, or something narrower still, or a
  docblock only, or nothing at all. One keeper declares it as a promoted constructor property, so
  the declaration and the assignment are the same line and no assignment statement exists in the
  file. One declares it in a trait, so the keeper is not a class. One field is public and is
  filled by the framework rather than by any constructor.
  What separates a good answer from a long one is the CHAIN. Several keepers are not holding a
  handle at all: their field holds something else, and that something else is the one holding it,
  one or two declarations further along, in a file the keeper never mentions and that contains no
  occurrence of the object's name, its interface's name, or anything to do with a store. Those
  are the rows that decide this answer, and reading the keeper's own file will not reveal them.
  Chains run to three links. Two more cases are worth catching because nothing about them looks
  like retention: a routine that duplicates a keeper and hands the copy the SAME live handle, so
  one handle ends up with two independent owners; and a keeper that hands a handle it is keeping
  to a second object which then keeps it too.
  Most of this layer sits in parts of the framework whose own subject has nothing to do with a
  store. The part of the tree obviously about the store holds the object itself and the
  collaborators it builds out of itself, and an answer dense there and silent elsewhere has
  enumerated inside one search scope and mistaken it for the framework.
  Every keeper must be pinned to an exact file:line on the field or the assignment that fills it,
  with the routine named, with the number of links to the store and each intermediate holder
  named, and with one phrase on what goes wrong for that keeper once a handle may no longer be
  kept.

steps:
- name: Map the database connection contract
  criteria:
    map_quality:
      weight: 0.4
      question: |
        Is the object traced as one connected story - constructed, built for a driver, resolved by
        name, memoised, stood up again after a drop, living for as long as the process - rather
        than listed as places a name is mentioned? The step asks for jobs along that story: what
        state one holds from construction; where one is actually built and who decides which
        specialised per-driver variant gets built; how a caller holding only a name ends up
        holding one, and what that lookup does the second time it is asked for the same name; what
        happens when the link underneath drops and a live one has to be stood up again; and how
        long one lives once it exists. The memoisation and the stand-up-again are the two that
        matter most here, because together they are exactly why keeping a handle is safe today and
        will not be after the change - an answer that locates both and says so has understood the
        ticket. The question is COVERAGE OF THE JOBS - all of them shown and connected is the full
        answer.
        The step then asks for the NARROWER PROMISE: the smaller declared thing this object
        satisfies and that most code handed one declares instead. Is it located at file:line, is
        what it promises and what it leaves out characterised, and does the answer draw the right
        consequence - that anything declaring only the smaller thing cannot see the rest of the
        object's abilities?
        The step finally asks for a deliberately SMALL third thing: the SHAPE of the working
        ground, not the roll of it. By what routes does one of these objects reach code that uses
        it, and who does the handing. Score whether the routes are genuinely distinct and
        correctly characterised. Do NOT reward an exhaustive enumeration of the places themselves:
        it was not asked for, and a long located list is not a better answer here than four
        accurate routes. Could the next step act from this orientation with targeted lookups
        rather than a fresh exploration?
    specificity:
      weight: 0.25
      question: |
        Does every place the answer names carry an exact file:line AND the name of the routine it
        lives in? A bare filename is not specific. A file:line with no routine named around it is
        not specific - the maintainer asked whose code it is, not where the character offset
        falls. A routine named without the file:line where it is defined is not specific either.
        Where the answer names construction, per-driver selection, resolution by name, memoisation
        or reconnection, is the line that actually constructs, selects, resolves, caches or
        reconnects located, and not just asserted from a class name or a familiar convention? Is
        the narrower promise located at its own declaration rather than named from memory?
    justification:
      weight: 0.2
      question: |
        Does each part of the lifecycle carry one phrase on what it contributes - what the
        constructor fixes, what decides the variant, what the repeat lookup returns instead of
        building again, what reconnection preserves and what it discards - and does the answer
        connect those facts to the lifetime the object actually has today, rather than restating
        the list of routines? Does it say what the narrower promise deliberately leaves out, and
        why that matters to code that declares it?
    uncertainty:
      weight: 0.15
      question: |
        Does the answer separate what it verified by reading from what it inferred from a name or
        from a familiar framework convention? This is a famous framework and its connection
        surface is the kind of thing a model can recite: manager memoisation, driver factories,
        savepoint naming and reconnection invite confident guessing, so saying which parts were
        actually opened is worth more here than elsewhere. Is anything the session could not
        resolve named as such rather than smoothed over, and are the routes offered as the ones it
        established rather than as a complete taxonomy it did not check?
- name: Audit every dependent of the database connection contract
  criteria:
    map_quality:
      weight: 0.4
      question: |
        COVERAGE AND CHAIN, and the two are one question here. Is every place that KEEPS one of
        these alive listed separately, each with the number of links to the store and each
        intermediate holder named? A keeper named with no chain beneath it is half a row.
        Score the DISTINCTION the ticket turns on: keeping versus using. A line that runs a
        statement, reaches a table or asks the object a question is NOT a retention line, however
        well located - it is the answer to a different question, and an answer built out of those
        lines has missed this one. What is being asked for is the field and the assignment that
        fills it. Equally, does the answer catch retention that does not look like a constructor
        assignment: a promoted constructor property where the declaration and the assignment are
        the same line; a field declared in a trait, so the keeper is not a class at all; a public
        field filled by the framework rather than by any constructor; a setter that replaces a
        kept chain wholesale after construction; a clone routine that duplicates a keeper and
        hands the copy the SAME live handle; a keeper that passes a handle it is keeping into a
        second object which then keeps it too.
        Score the CHAINS hardest. Several keepers hold something that holds the handle, one or two
        declarations further along, in a file that names neither the object nor a store; chains
        here run to three links. An answer that finds only the keepers whose own file mentions the
        object or its interface has found the shallow half and stopped. Does it follow each chain
        to its end and say how long it is?
        Score WHERE the answer looked. Most of this layer sits in parts of the framework whose own
        subject has nothing to do with a store. An answer dense in the part of the tree obviously
        about the store and thin elsewhere has enumerated inside one search scope and mistaken it
        for the framework; that is the exact failure this criterion names.
        Is the answer organised by area with each area's keepers listed individually, and is what
        was not reached said plainly rather than closed over?
    specificity:
      weight: 0.25
      question: |
        Does every keeper carry an exact file:line on the field or the assignment that fills it,
        AND the routine that line sits in, AND the chain length with each intermediate holder
        named, AND one phrase on the failure? All four, per row. A bare filename is not specific.
        A run of keepers named together under one shared reason with no location on any of them is
        the failure this criterion exists to catch, and it does not become specific because the
        reason is correct. Is the located line the retention line, rather than a later line where
        the kept handle is used? Where the answer names the local name a keeper chose for its
        field, is that the name the file actually uses rather than the one the contract itself
        uses?
    justification:
      weight: 0.2
      question: |
        Is the failure phrase per row a consequence of THAT keeper's retention, rather than a
        restatement that the place uses a store? "Breaks if the connection changes" is not a
        justification. "Holds the handle for the whole request and reaches it again at both ends
        of one", "the copy comes away holding the same live handle, so one handle acquires two
        owners", "the field is untyped, so what it holds is decided by whoever constructs it",
        "three links away, so nothing in this file will ever show the dependency" are. Does the
        answer say, at least in aggregate, which chains have to be re-plumbed and which keepers
        can simply re-resolve?
    uncertainty:
      weight: 0.15
      question: |
        Does the answer distinguish keepers it opened and read from ones it inferred from a name,
        a familiar convention, or a memory of this framework? That distinction matters more than
        usual here: this is a famous framework, and naming a plausible file without having read
        the field declaration is easy and looks the same as knowing. Chains especially invite
        guessing - asserting that one thing holds another because it usually does is not the same
        as having read the declaration that says so, and the answer should say which it did. Is
        coverage claimed honestly - which areas it swept and which it did not reach - and where a
        field's real contents were ambiguous, does it say so instead of picking one silently?
